The four new runs are all generating answers. The big finding this wake is that, on the new long-document set, the uncompressed reference was being cut short by the 32-token limit on answer length. Its score measured that limit, not how well it reads the document. I'm re-running the same rows with a 256-token limit.

## Done
- **Second judge check, finished:**
  - **10 pages vs 4 (documents):** no length loss, at both seeds. This holds under the standard judge prompt, the alternative Yes/No prompt and exact match. Every confidence interval stays within ±3 pt (1,500 question pairs per seed).
  - **20 pages vs 4:** the compressed model holds up better than the uncompressed one under all three scorers, by +4.3 to +7.7 pt. Only one seed has been run so far.
  - **40 passages vs 10 (text):** the result depends on the scorer (−2.6, −1.5, +0.8), so it stays undecided.
- **Trial runs on the new long-document set (300 rows each): healthy.** No errors, every row judged, and the compressed model's slot counts match the input audit on 300 of 300 rows.
- **The finding:** on these rows the uncompressed model scored *below* the compressed one (.34 vs .52).
  - It answers in full sentences, and the 32-token limit cuts 61–74% of its answers before the answer itself.
  - The compressed model is trained to answer briefly; only 8–17% of its answers are cut.
  - On the standard document test set this hits only 4% of uncompressed answers, so recorded results are barely affected.
- **Fix:** a small campaign-owned wrapper, `scripts/longdoc/eval_mnt.sbatch`. It runs a copy of the shared eval launcher with only the answer limit changed, and writes to new file names.
  - I did not edit the shared launcher. Other campaigns' evals were running, and the campaign rules forbid patching it then.
  - I added this rule to the campaign rules (CAMPAIGN.md): every run on the long-document set uses the 256-token limit.

## Running now
Four reruns of the same 300 rows at the 256-token limit (submission cap of 4 reached):
- uncompressed, pages
- uncompressed, text
- compressed model, pages
- compressed model, text (first compressed run on the long text set)

## Next
1. When the four runs end: check how many uncompressed answers still hit the 256-token limit (the rule is under 5%).
2. Launch the set checks: no-document runs and hidden-fact tests, plus a trial on the mixed text-and-image set.
3. Cost the full set of model runs from the measured run times. My rough estimate is ~50 GPU-hours against ~35 left (24 of 70 runs left). That would mean one budget question for you.

## Side notes
- The command guard refused a cleanup `rm -r` of a temp folder in /tmp, which it treats as deleting an output folder. I left the folder there and added my report to an existing complaint about the guard.
- The campaign's results web page shows 0 table rows; I did not look into it.

Nothing needed from you.
